  "learnLessonRulesTitle": "The Sudoku rules",
  "learnLessonRulesSummary": "Understand rows, columns, blocks, and the no-guessing mindset.",
  "learnLessonRulesBody": "Fill every empty cell with a digit from 1 to 9. Every row, every column, and every 3×3 block must contain each digit exactly once. A well-formed puzzle can be solved with logic: place a digit only when the current grid proves it.",
  "learnLessonRulesQuestion": "A 7 already appears in a cell's row. What follows?",
  "learnLessonRulesA": "The cell cannot contain 7",
  "learnLessonRulesB": "The cell must contain 7",
  "learnLessonRulesC": "The row no longer matters",
  "learnLessonRulesQ2": "The highlighted 3×3 block already contains 1 through 8. Which digit completes it?",
  "learnLessonRulesQ2A": "9",
  "learnLessonRulesQ2B": "Any digit missing from the row",
  "learnLessonRulesQ2C": "You have to guess",
  "learnLessonRulesQ2Why": "Every block contains 1 through 9 exactly once, so the only missing digit is 9.",
  "learnLessonCandidatesTitle": "Candidates and notes",
  "learnLessonCandidatesSummary": "Turn exclusions into a small, useful candidate list.",
  "learnLessonCandidatesBody": "A candidate is a digit that is not already excluded by the cell's row, column, or block. Notes are working information, not guesses. Update them when a placement removes a possibility nearby.",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQuestion": "When should a digit be written as a candidate?",
  "learnLessonCandidatesA": "Whenever it looks likely",
  "learnLessonCandidatesB": "Only when row, column, and block allow it",
  "learnLessonCandidatesC": "Only after making a guess",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQ2": "The highlighted cell sees 1 and 2 in its row, 3 and 4 in its column, and 5 and 6 in its block. Which candidates remain?",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQ2A": "7, 8, and 9",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQ2B": "1, 2, and 3",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQ2C": "4, 5, and 6",
  "learnLessonCandidatesQ2Why": "Only 7, 8, and 9 survive all three checks. Candidates are possibilities, not guesses.",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleSummary": "Find a cell with exactly one candidate left.",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleBody": "If eight digits are excluded from one cell, its remaining candidate is forced. This is a naked single: the answer is visible directly in that cell's candidate list.",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQuestion": "A cell has only candidate 4. What is the logical move?",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleA": "Enter 4",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleB": "Erase the note 4",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleC": "Wait for a second candidate",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQ2": "The highlighted cell has candidates 3 and 8. Is this already a naked single?",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQ2A": "No, two possibilities remain",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQ2B": "Yes, enter 3",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQ2C": "Yes, enter 8",
  "learnLessonNakedSingleQ2Why": "A naked single requires exactly one remaining candidate. With two candidates, more information is needed.",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleSummary": "Find the only place for a digit inside one unit.",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleBody": "A cell may have several candidates, yet one of them can be unique within its row, column, or block. If 6 appears as a candidate in only one cell of that unit, 6 is forced there.",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQuestion": "In a block, only one cell can contain 6. That cell also allows 2. What can you place?",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleA": "Nothing, because it has two notes",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleB": "6, because it has the only place in the block",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleC": "2, because it is smaller",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQ2": "Why is 6 forced in the highlighted cell even though that cell also allows 2?",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQ2A": "It is the only cell in the row that allows 6",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQ2B": "6 is always stronger than 2",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQ2C": "The highlighted cell must use its largest candidate",
  "learnLessonHiddenSingleQ2Why": "Look digit-first: every other cell in the row excludes 6, so this is 6's only place.",
  "learnLessonLockedSummary": "Use the overlap between a block and a row or column.",
  "learnLessonLockedBody": "If every candidate for a digit in a block lies on the same row, that digit is locked into the block-row intersection. Remove it from the rest of that row. The same logic works with columns.",
  "learnLessonLockedQuestion": "All possible 5s in a block lie in row 3. Where can 5 be removed?",
  "learnLessonLockedA": "From the rest of row 3 outside that block",
  "learnLessonLockedB": "From every cell in the block",
  "learnLessonLockedC": "From all other rows",
  "learnLessonLockedQ2": "In the highlighted row, every possible 4 lies inside the middle block. Where can 4 be removed?",
  "learnLessonLockedQ2A": "From the other cells of that block",
  "learnLessonLockedQ2B": "From the entire highlighted row",
  "learnLessonLockedQ2C": "Nowhere; a placement is required first",
  "learnLessonLockedQ2Why": "This is claiming: the row claims its 4 inside one block, so the block cannot contain 4 outside that row.",
  "learnLessonPairsTitle": "Pairs",
  "learnLessonPairsSummary": "Reserve two digits for two cells.",
  "learnLessonPairsBody": "A naked pair is two cells in one unit containing the same two candidates; those digits can be removed from other cells in the unit. A hidden pair is two digits that occur only in the same two cells; other notes can be removed from those cells.",
  "learnLessonPairsQuestion": "Two cells in a row both contain only 2 and 8. What follows?",
  "learnLessonPairsA": "2 and 8 can be removed from the other cells in that row",
  "learnLessonPairsB": "Both cells must be 2",
  "learnLessonPairsC": "The pair has no effect",
  "learnLessonPairsQ2": "Only the two highlighted cells in a column can contain 4 or 7. They also contain other notes. What is the hidden-pair move?",
  "learnLessonPairsQ2A": "Keep only 4 and 7 in those two cells",
  "learnLessonPairsQ2B": "Remove 4 and 7 from those two cells",
  "learnLessonPairsQ2C": "Place 4 in both cells",
  "learnLessonPairsQ2Why": "The two cells are reserved for 4 and 7 in some order, so their other candidates can be removed.",
  "learnLessonTriplesTitle": "Triples",
  "learnLessonTriplesSummary": "Extend subset logic from two cells to three.",
  "learnLessonTriplesBody": "Three cells in one unit can reserve exactly three digits even when not every cell shows all three. For a naked triple, the union of their candidates has size three. Hidden triples use the inverse view: three digits occur nowhere else in the unit.",
  "learnLessonTriplesQuestion": "Three cells in one row use only the combined candidates 1, 4, and 9. What may you do?",
  "learnLessonTriplesA": "Remove 1, 4, and 9 from the row's other cells",
  "learnLessonTriplesB": "Put all three digits into each cell",
  "learnLessonTriplesC": "Remove every other candidate from the row",
  "learnLessonTriplesQ2": "In one block, the digits 2, 5, and 6 occur only in three highlighted cells. What does the hidden triple allow?",
  "learnLessonTriplesQ2A": "Remove other candidates from those three cells",
  "learnLessonTriplesQ2B": "Remove 2, 5, and 6 from those three cells",
  "learnLessonTriplesQ2C": "Place all three digits immediately",
  "learnLessonTriplesQ2Why": "Those three cells must contain 2, 5, and 6 in some order. Notes outside that set can be cleared from the three cells.",
  "learnLessonWingsTitle": "Wings: X-Wing and XY-Wing",
  "learnLessonWingsSummary": "Read linked candidate patterns across multiple units.",
  "learnLessonWingsBody": "An X-Wing uses one digit in two rows and the same two columns, allowing eliminations in those columns. An XY-Wing uses three two-candidate cells: a pivot sees two wings, and either pivot value forces the shared wing candidate. These are elimination patterns, not guesses.",
  "learnLessonWingsQuestion": "Candidate 5 forms the four corners of the highlighted rectangle across two rows and two columns. What is the X-Wing conclusion?",
  "learnLessonWingsA": "Remove 5 from other cells in the two columns",
  "learnLessonWingsB": "Place 5 in all four corners",
  "learnLessonWingsC": "Remove every other candidate from the corners",
  "learnLessonWingsQ2": "The pivot has 2/3; its wings have 2/7 and 3/7. What can a cell that sees both wings lose?",
  "learnLessonWingsQ2A": "2",
  "learnLessonWingsQ2B": "3",
  "learnLessonWingsQ2C": "7",
  "learnLessonWingsQ2Why": "Whichever value the pivot takes, one wing must become 7. A cell seeing both wings therefore cannot be 7."
}
